        <section id="document-tools"  class="t-bg-supporting3">
          
            <h2>Document Tools</h2>
              <h3>Document tools, Live documentation / Automation</h3>
<p>Most development environments now include a document tool which would often be used as a <strong>Knowledge Management Tool</strong>.  The <strong>knowledge base</strong> would be updated as the software is built, bugs are fixed, new features and enhancements are added to the software, and new versions of the software application are rolled out. Take a look below to see some examples.</p>

              
              
  <div class="toggle-group">
  
<toggle-section open="false">
    <h3>Examples of document tools</h3>
    <h3>GitHub</h3>
<p>The knowledge base would be managed in different ways according to the type of document tool used. For example, <strong>GitHub</strong> would treat documents as artefacts so they would have their own document folders with their own repository and can be published to their own website page.</p>
<h3>Tettra</h3>
<p>Another tool - <strong>Tettra</strong>, is a flexible document tool which manages the knowledge base to share timely and accurate information amongst team members. It would create a catalog with a Q&amp;A format which can be accesses via a collaboration tool such as Slack.</p>

</toggle-section>
  
<toggle-section open="false">
    <h3>Automatic documentation tool</h3>
    <p>Documentation could also be automated. A system could be set to automatically generate documents each time a change is made to the software so it is always up to date. The tool could be used with a focus on:</p>
<ul>
<li>High level view with a description of requirements</li>
<li>The data model</li>
<li>Cross references between functionalities, functions, and data</li>
<li>Business rules</li>
<li>Subsystem description and interaction</li>
<li>Comments</li>
</ul>

</toggle-section>
</div>

            
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
